# Every setting hi reads, one row each, in the order docs/SETTINGS.md's
# _Every setting_ table lists them: the table is written from these rows
# (.github/prettier-plugin-docs.mjs), and `hi --configure` reads its yes/no
# items from them (scripts/configure.sh's _hi_settings_load).
#
# <section> | <name> | <default> | <off> | <on> | <preview> | <needs> | <label> | <what it does>
#
# <section> is where the wizard asks: feature, header, prompt, or advanced
# for a menu item, value for a value it writes, you for a setting nothing
# asks about (the table's "set by" column follows from it). <off>, <on>,
# <preview>, <needs>, and <label> are a yes/no item's, as
# _hi_settings_load describes them, and - on a row that is no item.
# <default> and <what it does> are the table's cells, in Markdown. No column
# holds a ` | `; a `|` inside one is written `\|`.

header | _HI_DISABLE_BANNER | `0` | 1 | - | - | - | banner - the === Connected [host] === line, always first | hides the `=== Connected ===` line ([Header details](#header-details))
feature | _HI_DISABLE_HEADER | `0` | 1 | - | - | - | connect/disconnect header - off hides every row below | turns off the whole header: a connect's, a disconnect's, and the greeting a local interactive shell prints; a connect then leaves `header.sh` and the package list out of the payload
feature | _HI_DISABLE_GREETING | `0` | 1 | - | - | - | greeting - the "hi loaded:" line and its timers | hides the `hi loaded:` line and its init/copy/load timers; not part of the header, so `_HI_DISABLE_HEADER` leaves it alone ([Header details](#header-details))
value | _HI_HEADER_ORDER | the table in [Header details](#header-details) | - | - | - | - | - | which header items show, in what order; empty is the default list
value | _HI_MAX_WIDTH | `80` | - | - | - | - | - | terminal columns the header and banner are drawn to, narrowed to a smaller real terminal; 40 is the least the wizard takes
value | _HI_PACKAGES_GROUPS | `core useful deprecated` | - | - | - | - | - | the `config/packages` groups the header's check runs, space- or comma-separated, or `none`, and the main dial on its length: `base` adds the tools hi itself leans on, and a file started from `docs/packages.example` has `extras`, `trivia`, and `platform` besides. A group of your own is named the same way; drop `check` from `_HI_HEADER_ORDER` to turn the check off. See [The package check's rows](COLORS.md#the-package-checks-rows)
value | _HI_IP_HIDE | `172.*` | - | - | - | - | - | globs the header's `ip` cell drops; `none` hides nothing ([Header details](#header-details))
feature | _HI_DISABLE_GIT_STATUS | `0` | 1 | - | _hi_git_status_preview | - | git status - the branch and its changes | turns off the git segment in the prompt
feature | _HI_DISABLE_ENV_STATUS | `0` | 1 | - | _hi_env_status_preview | - | environment segment - (myproj) for a venv, ... | turns off the prompt's environment segment - the leading `(myproj)` naming the active venv, conda, direnv, nix, guix, devbox, or version-manager environment. See [Integrations](INTEGRATIONS.md#the-environment-segment)
feature | _HI_TOOL_ALIASES | `0` | - | 1 | _hi_tool_alias_preview | - | styled tool aliases - ls -> eza, cat -> bat | `1` turns on the styled tool aliases: `cat`/`catn` as `bat` with `_HI_BAT_OPTS`, `bat`/`batcat`/`batn`, and `ls`/`exa`/`eza` through the list ladder. Off, none of them exists and the `_HI_*_BIN` lookups are skipped. See [Integrations](INTEGRATIONS.md#bat-and-eza)
feature | _HI_SUDO_ALIAS | `0` | - | 1 | - | - | sudo alias - aliases survive under sudo | `1` turns on the `sudo` alias - the trailing-space alias in bash/zsh that lets `sudo vim` keep the vim alias's flags, and fish's wrapper function that does the same for its alias functions
feature | _HI_DISABLE_LOCAL | `0` | 1 | - | - | - | this machine - hi styles its own shells, not only where you hi | turns off everything above, the two alias opt-ins included whatever they say, and the banner, **on this machine only** - hi still styles the hosts you visit ([Others](#others))
prompt | _HI_DISABLE_PROMPT | `0` | 1 | - | _hi_prompt_preview | - | colored user@host prompt - off, your shell's own draws | turns off the colored `user@host` prompt, leaving your shell's own
value | _HI_PROMPT_TOOL | unset | - | - | - | - | - | who draws the prompt: a space-separated list of `powerlevel10k`, `oh-my-zsh`, `oh-my-bash`, `bash-it`, `tide`, `starship`, `oh-my-posh`, `powerline-go`, and `hi` (hi's own), each shell taking the first entry that fits it and that the target has. An entry written `<shell>:<program>` (`bash:starship`) is tried first, by that shell alone, so `bash:starship hi` draws starship in bash and hi's prompt in zsh and fish. With no plain entry, or unset, the rest is every program installed on this machine, frameworks first, so a prompt you already use follows you; `hi` keeps hi's prompt everywhere. `hi --configure` asks shell by shell. hi keeps its header and aliases either way, and installs nothing. See [Integrations](INTEGRATIONS.md#prompt-programs)
value | _HI_PROMPT_END_BASH | `\$` | - | - | - | - | - | bash's prompt separator (`\$` is bash's escape for "`$`, or `#` for root"); also the plain `sh` prompt hi bakes on the client for a bash-less target
value | _HI_PROMPT_END_ZSH | `>` | - | - | - | - | - | zsh's prompt separator - zsh prompt escapes work, so `%#` behaves as anywhere else in `PS1`
value | _HI_PROMPT_END_FISH | `\|` | - | - | - | - | - | fish's prompt separator; unset, root gets `#` in its place, and a value you set is used for root too
advanced | _HI_DISABLE_LEAD_SPACE | `0` | 0 | 1 | - | - | drop the leading space - before the prompt and header lines | `1` drops the leading space before the prompt's `user@host`, the git segment, the banner line, and the first cell of every header row
advanced | _HI_DISABLE_RIGHT_EDGE | `0` | 0 | 1 | - | - | drop the header's right edge - every row ends at its last cell | `1` drops the closing \| from every header row and the greeting line, so each ends at its last cell instead of at the banner's column
value | _HI_PLUGINS_ON | unset | - | - | - | - | - | the plugins or groups that are off by default (`default = "off"` in their table: the shipped `hooks`, whose tools keep state on a target) and should ride and run anyway, a space or a comma apart; `hi --plugin-on` and `hi --plugin-off` write it ([Switching a plugin off](#switching-a-plugin-off))
value | _HI_PLUGINS_OFF | unset | - | - | - | - | - | the plugins, groups, or members that stay home, a space or a comma apart; `hi --plugin-off` and `hi --plugin-on` write it too ([Switching a plugin off](#switching-a-plugin-off))
advanced | _HI_MUX | `0` | 0 | 1 | - | - | default every connect to --mux - a local tmux, zellij, or screen session | `1` makes every connect a `--mux` one, in a local tmux, zellij, or screen session; `--no-mux` overrides it for one connect
advanced | _HI_KEEP | `0` | 0 | 1 | - | - | default ssh connects to --keep - a tmux, zellij, or screen session on the target | `1` makes every ssh connect a `--keep` one: the session runs in tmux, zellij, or screen on the target and outlives the connection ([Integrations](INTEGRATIONS.md#terminal-multiplexers)); `--no-keep` overrides it for one connect
you | _HI_UPDATE_SIGNED | `0` | - | - | - | - | - | `1` has `hi --update` refuse a release tag unless its signature verifies against a key the checkout already trusts (`.github/allowed_signers`, or your gpg keyring); unset, only a signature that fails to verify refuses, and an unsigned tag or an unknown key is said and allowed ([Security](SECURITY.md#what-hi-does---and-deliberately-doesnt))
feature | _HI_PLAIN | `0` | 1 | - | - | - | targets - hi styles the hosts and containers you hi to | `1` makes every connect a `--plain` one, a bare shell with nothing sent; `--no-plain` overrides it for one connect. The wizard's targets switch writes it for every target; in a tag's file, `settings.<tag>.sh`, it holds for the hosts carrying that tag ([Settings by host tag](#settings-by-host-tag))
you | _HI_KEEP_TIMEOUT | `24h` | - | - | - | - | - | how long a kept session waits with nobody attached before it closes and its directory is removed: a number of seconds, or one with `s`, `m`, `h`, or `d`; `0` is never. Read on the target, so it belongs in `settings.sh`
you | _HI_KEEP_RETRY | `5m` | - | - | - | - | - | how long a connect inside a local tmux, zellij, or screen keeps retrying a target whose kept session it lost the link to: a duration as above; `0` is never
you | _HI_BACKENDS_OFF | unset | - | - | - | - | - | the backends hi never asks, a space or a comma apart: any of `docker`, `podman`, `nerdctl`, `finch`, `nomad`, and `kube`, or `all` for ssh alone. One named here is left out of target resolution, `hi <TAB>`, and the header's counts, so its CLI is never started; `--use <backend>` still reaches it by name ([Compatibility](COMPATIBILITY.md#what-ships))
you | _HI_DISABLE_CONTROLMASTER | `0` | - | - | - | - | - | `1` keeps hi from passing ssh its `ControlMaster`, `ControlPath`, and `ControlPersist` options, so a `ControlMaster` line of your own `~/.ssh/config` decides; the bootstrap and the session are then two connections, each authenticating ([Security](SECURITY.md#trust-boundaries))
advanced | _HI_TRUECOLOR | by terminal | - | - | - | - | - | `1`/`0` forces or refuses 24-bit color; unset, the client decides ([Colors](COLORS.md))
you | _HI_EDITOR | unset | - | - | - | - | - | the editor a target session exports as `$EDITOR`, `$VISUAL`, and `$SUDO_EDITOR`, by command name (`nvim`, `micro`, ...); used when the target has it. Unset, `$EDITOR` is your own `$EDITOR`'s command (else your `$VISUAL`'s) and `$VISUAL` your `$VISUAL`'s (else your `$EDITOR`'s) where the target has it, else the first of `nvim vim micro hx nano emacs` it does have; `$SUDO_EDITOR` follows `$EDITOR`. Each carries hi's config flags so `git commit` and `sudo -e` get the editor the alias gives you
you | _HI_PACKAGES_PALETTE | unset | - | - | - | - | - | the color the check paints each group's tier in: eight color names, four installed then four missing. Unset, or anything but eight names, is the shipped ramp (`cyan green brcyan brgreen blue magenta bryellow brred`). See [The package check's ramp](COLORS.md#the-package-checks-ramp)
you | _HI_COLOR_SCHEME | unset | - | - | - | - | - | what the palette names render as on a terminal that reports 24-bit color: twenty-four or forty-eight six-digit hex words. Unset is the terminal's own sixteen colors. See [Colors](COLORS.md)
you | _HI_POWERLINE_GO_OPTS | unset | - | - | - | - | - | extra flags for [powerline-go](https://github.com/justjanne/powerline-go) when it draws the prompt, word-split (`-modules venv,cwd,git -mode flat`)
you | _HI_SYMBOL_SSH | `»` (`>` in ASCII) | - | - | - | - | - | the symbol an ssh host carries in `hi <TAB>`'s list - fish's description column, beside the name when bash 4+ lists matches, zsh's display. Any text; an empty value counts as unset
you | _HI_SYMBOL_CONTAINER | `▣` (`#`) | - | - | - | - | - | the same for a docker, podman, nerdctl, or finch container
you | _HI_SYMBOL_NOMAD | `◆` (`*`) | - | - | - | - | - | the same for a nomad allocation
you | _HI_SYMBOL_KUBE | `⎈` (`@`) | - | - | - | - | - | the same for a kubernetes pod
you | NO_COLOR | unset | - | - | - | - | - | not hi's variable but [the convention](https://no-color.org): any non-empty value renders everything without color, shipped to the target next to [`_HI_ASCII`](#not-settings)
you | _HI_LS_OPTS | the rung's own flags below, else `-F -l` (and `--color=auto` where `ls` takes it) | - | - | - | - | - | the flags the `ls`/`eza`/`exa` alias runs with - set it and it is the whole answer, whichever binary the ladder picked
you | _HI_EZA_OPTS | `-F -1 -l -m --group-directories-first --smart-group` + a time format | - | - | - | - | - | what `_HI_LS_OPTS` defaults to when eza is the rung that answered
you | _HI_EXA_OPTS | the same leading flags + `--group --no-filesize` | - | - | - | - | - | the same for exa, whose column set its successor dropped
you | _HI_BAT_OPTS | `-P --tabs 2 --style changes,grid` | - | - | - | - | - | the flags the `bat`/`batn` aliases attach; no `--theme`, so your bat config's theme is the one you see
you | _HI_CAT_BIN | first of `bat`, `batcat`, `ccat`, `cat` on PATH | - | - | - | - | - | which binary the `bat` and `cat` aliases run (Debian ships bat as `batcat`; the tail keeps `cat` working where none is installed). Like the other two `_BIN`s, looked up only under `_HI_TOOL_ALIASES=1`
you | _HI_BAT_BIN | first of `bat`, `batcat` on PATH | - | - | - | - | - | the bat-only tier behind it, what parses `_HI_BAT_OPTS` - two rungs shorter on purpose; set with it, or leave both alone
you | _HI_LS_BIN | first of `eza`, `exa`, `ls` on PATH | - | - | - | - | - | which binary the `ls`, `eza`, and `exa` aliases all run - one ladder, and `_HI_LS_OPTS` follows the rung it answered with
